    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d360">

                <pb facs="0472" n="404" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg404"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72/1758¾</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-05-14">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">14 May, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I was as sorry as you
                    can have been to withdraw the “statement” which we had intended for the
                        press.<anchor n="61" xml:id="d360-f1"/> It cost me a struggle to do so. But
                    the intimation was plain from the German Embassy (and I cannot doubt the source
                    of my information) that we were not in earnest, would speak only in a
                    Pickwickian sense if we seemed to speak with firmness, and I did not dare lend
                    colour to that impression. You will notice that hope of a pacific settlement was
                    expressed. That, in the circumstances, was as far as I dared to go.</p>
                <p>Faithfully Yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. W.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
